<p>Vietnam imported 33,008 MT of Indian fish meal worth USD 40.5 million in calendar 2024, the second-largest destination after China (USD 93.1 million, 70,344 MT) among India's USD 258.0 million total fish meal exports worldwide. The driver is straightforward: Vietnam operates one of the world's largest aquaculture sectors, with farmed shrimp and pangasius (basa) catfish production requiring a steady, quality-consistent supply of protein-rich feed ingredients. This guide is for Vietnamese feed mills and ingredient importers: which parameters to specify, how testing and packing are handled, what moves the price against Peruvian supply, and where first orders go wrong.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<h2>What Vietnamese feed mills actually specify</h2>
<p>Fish meal buyers, in our experience, ask more precise technical questions than most agricultural commodity buyers, and for good reason — feed formulation is a precision exercise. The parameters that matter:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Protein content</strong> — commonly 60% or higher for premium aquafeed applications, lower grades for other livestock feed use</li>
  <li><strong>Freshness indicators</strong> — total volatile nitrogen (TVN) and histamine levels, which reflect how the raw fish was handled before processing began</li>
  <li><strong>Fat content</strong> — affects both nutritional value and storage stability</li>
  <li><strong>Salt content</strong> — relevant for feed formulation balance</li>
</ul>
<p>Tell us your target protein grade and any freshness thresholds your feed formulation requires, and we'll quote against your actual specification.</p>

<h2>Testing and batch-to-batch consistency</h2>
<p>Because fish meal enters a feed chain that ultimately reaches food-producing animals, we treat microbiological testing (salmonella in particular) and adulteration checks as standard practice rather than an optional extra. {{ site.inspectionStatement }}</p>
<p>A fish meal shipment that varies noticeably from the last one — even within nominal specification tolerances — creates real problems for a mill running standardised feed formulas. We aim for batch-to-batch consistency within your agreed specification, and can supply certificates of analysis per shipment so your own quality team can track variation over time rather than discovering a drift only when finished feed performance changes.</p>

<h2>Packaging and storage</h2>
<p>Standard export packing is 25kg or 50kg PP bags, with bulk options available for larger feed-mill contracts. Fish meal needs dry, cool storage — moisture ingress degrades both quality and shelf life, so packaging and transit handling matter as much as the initial specification.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>The Certificate of Free Sale is the line in this checklist that needs the most lead time on a first order — Vietnam has required it for feed ingredients including fish meal since March 2020, so confirm it early rather than once the shipment is otherwise ready.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>We don't quote a flat per-tonne number for fish meal, because the raw material behind it doesn't move on a flat basis either. Five things matter most: crude protein grade (65% minimum commands a real premium over 60% minimum, since higher-protein aquafeed formulations need less inclusion rate per tonne of finished feed), freshness/TVN reading (fresher raw material — lower TVN — is worth more, since it indicates the fish wasn't degraded before processing began), seasonal raw-material supply from India's coastal fish-landing regions (by-catch and trimmings volume tracks each region's fishing season, not a single harvest window, so availability and cost shift through the year), competition from Peruvian fishmeal (the dominant global benchmark origin, which sets a reference price even though India competes on freight advantage into Southeast Asia rather than on global scale), and prevailing INR/USD/VND exchange rates plus freight cost to Ho Chi Minh City or Haiphong.</p>
<p>On that last point, Peru remains the world's dominant fish meal exporter by volume, so India isn't competing globally on scale. What India offers Vietnamese buyers specifically is a freight and logistics advantage on this particular route — shorter transit and lower shipping cost than sourcing from South America, which matters for a feed input where landed cost per protein unit is the key economics.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific protein grade, freshness tolerance, packaging and destination port — never as a generic per-tonne average. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: protein minimum (60% or 65%), any TVN/freshness ceiling your formulation requires, packaging preference, quantity, and destination port.</p>
