<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The most common dispute on fish meal shipments is a protein-content shortfall against contract — this is occasionally a genuine quality issue, but more often it's a basis mismatch (protein reported on an as-is versus a moisture-adjusted basis can differ meaningfully, and if the purchase order doesn't state which basis applies, both labs can report correctly and still disagree). State the basis explicitly in your order, not just the target percentage.</p>
<p>The second common issue is a TVN or histamine reading outside expected range on arrival, which points to raw material that sat too long — or was handled at too high a temperature — before processing began. This is a genuine freshness/quality signal, not a paperwork formality: a mill that doesn't check it can end up with feed-conversion problems traced back weeks later to a shipment nobody flagged at the time. We test TVN as standard practice and would recommend insisting on it in every certificate of analysis, from any supplier.</p>
<p>Third, salt content above tolerance is a real, specific issue for feed formulation balance — fish meal processed with less care around brine/wash steps can carry excess salt that throws off a mill's finished-feed sodium target. Specify your salt ceiling if your formulation is sensitive to it.</p>
<p>What first-time Vietnamese buyers most often get wrong: underestimating how long the Certificate of Free Sale (CFS) requirement takes to arrange on a first order, and treating it as a formality rather than a genuine lead-time item. Build CFS confirmation into your order timeline from day one, not as an afterthought once the shipment is otherwise ready.</p>
<!-- operator-detail:end -->

